My wheels.

1032
00:32:57,350 --> 00:32:58,519
Let me spin them.

1033
00:32:58,519 --> 00:32:59,767
-Hm?

1034
00:32:59,767 --> 00:33:01,484
-There's got to be
some way to help you.

1035
00:33:01,484 --> 00:33:03,342
-Max, these people
won't even talk to me.

1036
00:33:03,342 --> 00:33:04,700
They say I'm trouble.

1037
00:33:04,700 --> 00:33:05,788
-Maxi Priest?

1038
00:33:05,788 --> 00:33:07,785
-There's supposed to be
a meeting this afternoon.

1039
00:33:07,785 --> 00:33:09,852
But I'll be sitting alone.
-Oh yeah?

1040
00:33:09,852 --> 00:33:10,811
What kind of meeting?

1041
00:33:10,811 --> 00:33:12,289
-A union meeting.

1042
00:33:12,289 --> 00:33:13,567
-Oh, they going to have cookies?

1043
00:33:13,567 --> 00:33:14,266
-Meeting.

1044
00:33:14,266 --> 00:33:15,574
Or any union.

1045
00:33:15,574 --> 00:33:16,802
Get out of my cannery.

1046
00:33:16,802 --> 00:33:18,620
-So you're canning her.
-D'oh!

1047
00:33:18,620 --> 00:33:21,455
-How ironic.

1048
00:33:21,455 --> 00:33:22,853
-You want to take a
vote on that, Webster?

1049
00:33:22,853 --> 00:33:25,230
-Uh, Max, this is a cannery.

1050
00:33:25,230 --> 00:33:26,788
-You've got a lot of
people working here.

1051
00:33:29,454 --> 00:33:30,982
I'd say they outvote you 100--

1052
00:33:30,982 --> 00:33:33,119
-(SINGING) "Look for
the union label."

1053
00:33:33,119 --> 00:33:34,357
Come on, everybody.

1054
00:33:34,357 --> 00:33:35,445
Come on, Pete Seeger, come on.

1055
00:33:35,445 --> 00:33:36,394
Weavers, join in.

1056
00:33:36,394 --> 00:33:39,509
Come on, Woody.

1057
00:33:39,509 --> 00:33:41,067
-Can't stop people from
meeting and talking.

1058
00:33:41,067 --> 00:33:42,265
-I told you to get out.

1059
00:33:42,265 --> 00:33:43,024
-Throw me out.

1060
00:33:43,024 --> 00:33:44,901
-Ooh.

1061
00:33:44,901 --> 00:33:47,048
-Loud talk doesn't scare anyone.

1062
00:33:47,048 --> 00:33:49,565
-Old Black Eyes is back.

1063
00:33:49,565 --> 00:33:51,013
-You should attend.

1064
00:33:51,013 --> 00:33:52,191
It's your future, too.

1065
00:33:55,047 --> 00:33:56,714
-My hero.

1066
00:33:56,714 --> 00:33:57,543
-Mine too.

1067
00:33:57,543 --> 00:34:00,079
-Now I know that none of what
I've said this afternoon is--

1068
00:34:00,079 --> 00:34:01,378
-Oh, she's an Alpha Omega.

1069
00:34:01,378 --> 00:34:01,947
We hate them.

1070
00:34:01,947 --> 00:34:03,914
-Goodness, most of y'all have
been working here much longer

1071
00:34:03,914 --> 00:34:05,422
than I have.

1072
00:34:05,422 --> 00:34:08,657
Your mothers and your
fathers worked here.

1073
00:34:08,657 --> 00:34:10,654
-Dominus vobiscum, everyone.

1074
00:34:10,654 --> 00:34:13,031
-But now we have
to make a change.

1075
00:34:13,031 --> 00:34:14,728
To make a life for us.

1076
00:34:14,728 --> 00:34:16,695
For your kids.

1077
00:34:16,695 --> 00:34:19,481
Now I've got a petition
here for the union.

1078
00:34:19,481 --> 00:34:21,708
And I need 40 more signatures.

1079
00:34:21,708 --> 00:34:23,615
So let's see a show of hands.

1080
00:34:23,615 --> 00:34:25,483
-I lost my hand
in a fish chopper.

1081
00:34:33,451 --> 00:34:35,348
-Hm.

1082
00:34:35,348 --> 00:34:36,776
-Heh heh heh.

1083
00:34:36,776 --> 00:34:38,843
-Studs Terkel, not working.
-All this?

1084
00:34:38,843 --> 00:34:40,241
Huh, Carrie?

1085
00:34:40,241 --> 00:34:41,380
-Charlie.

1086
00:34:41,380 --> 00:34:42,158
-Um.

1087
00:34:42,158 --> 00:34:44,096
-I have heard the stories
of the Thugee cult.

1088
00:34:44,096 --> 00:34:46,432
-I can't afford to
give up one day's pay.

1089
00:34:46,432 --> 00:34:48,669
Let alone, paycheck.

1090
00:34:48,669 --> 00:34:49,947
None of us can.

1091
00:34:49,947 --> 00:34:50,966
-Howard Johnson's right!

1092
00:34:50,966 --> 00:34:53,961
-[MURMURING]

1093
00:34:53,961 --> 00:34:55,789
-You know where my
family's at, Carrie.

1094
00:34:55,789 --> 00:34:59,264
-At the trailer park
at the edge of town.

1095
00:34:59,264 --> 00:35:01,690
-Till Bill gets his health back.
-Delta Burke?

1096
00:35:04,676 --> 00:35:07,432
-I tried applying for a
job at your cannery today.

1097
00:35:07,432 --> 00:35:08,730
Couldn't get in.

1098
00:35:08,730 --> 00:35:10,388
-I had no marketable skills.

1099
00:35:10,388 --> 00:35:11,885
-I'm not surprised, though.

1100
00:35:11,885 --> 00:35:13,703
Working conditions
there are so great.

1101
00:35:13,703 --> 00:35:15,730
-Anybody "umberstand" him?

1102
00:35:15,730 --> 00:35:17,128
-How much? $300?

1103
00:35:17,128 --> 00:35:18,076
-Hey, he's doing stand-up.

1104
00:35:18,076 --> 00:35:19,844
He's working the crowd.

1105
00:35:19,844 --> 00:35:21,911
-Looking forward to your
children going to college?

1106
00:35:21,911 --> 00:35:23,469
Because it's not going
to be easy on the salary

1107
00:35:23,469 --> 00:35:24,118
you're making.

1108
00:35:24,118 --> 00:35:26,874
But when the cost-of-living
increases come in every year,

1109
00:35:26,874 --> 00:35:29,210
that'll make things
a whole lot easier.

1110
00:35:29,210 --> 00:35:30,379
I don't know why
Carrie's bothering

1111
00:35:30,379 --> 00:35:32,845
to rock the boat for you people.
You're doing just fine.

1112
00:35:32,845 --> 00:35:34,672
-He's speaking in tongues.

1113
00:35:34,672 --> 00:35:36,410
-You get paid what
they want to give you.

1114
00:35:36,410 --> 00:35:38,607
You work the hours
they tell you.

1115
00:35:38,607 --> 00:35:41,183
You clock in and punch
out right on time.

1116
00:35:41,183 --> 00:35:41,882
Their time!

1117
00:35:41,882 --> 00:35:44,638
-Hey, time is truly a
subjective phenomenon, Tim.

1118
00:35:44,638 --> 00:35:46,226
-Carrie's waiting
for a show of hands.

1119
00:35:46,226 --> 00:35:48,323
She isn't going to get one.

1120
00:35:48,323 --> 00:35:49,601
-Should we have a silent vote?

1121
00:35:49,601 --> 00:35:51,378
-Big Brother isn't watching you.

1122
00:35:51,378 --> 00:35:52,656
He's got you right
in his hip pocket.

1123
00:35:52,656 --> 00:35:53,026
-Huh.

1124
00:35:53,026 --> 00:35:54,354
-Well, I don't have
a problem with that.

1125
00:35:54,354 --> 00:35:55,482
-If you ask me,
you're all getting

1126
00:35:55,482 --> 00:35:56,551
exactly what you deserve.

1127
00:35:56,551 --> 00:35:59,317
-We didn't ask.

1128
00:35:59,317 --> 00:36:02,043
-Now everyone, take
out your hymnal.

1129
00:36:02,043 --> 00:36:08,723
[APPLAUSE]

1130
00:36:08,723 --> 00:36:09,692
-I'll sign.

1131
00:36:09,692 --> 00:36:12,098
-You're fired.
-I'll sign.

1132
00:36:12,098 --> 00:36:13,246
-You're fired.
-You're fired.

1133
00:36:13,246 --> 00:36:14,485
-You're fired, too.

1134
00:36:14,485 --> 00:36:16,452
-We feel confident, Secure.

1135
00:36:20,476 --> 00:36:21,255
-Attica!

1136
00:36:21,255 --> 00:36:22,453
Attica!

1137
00:36:22,453 --> 00:36:23,402
Attica!

1138
00:36:23,402 --> 00:36:25,139
Attica!

1139
00:36:25,139 --> 00:36:26,467
-Mmm, Tahitian Treat.

1140
00:36:26,467 --> 00:36:28,594
Hope the meeting
didn't start yet.

1141
00:36:28,594 --> 00:36:30,402
Boy, it's cold.

1142
00:36:30,402 --> 00:36:31,280
[BURP]

1143
00:36:31,280 --> 00:36:32,788
-Oh, yahtzee.
Oh, hi.

1144
00:36:32,788 --> 00:36:34,765
What's up, gang?

1145
00:36:34,765 --> 00:36:36,822
-You took the last
Tahitian Treat, old man.

1146
00:36:36,822 --> 00:36:38,130
-Jimmy Carl Black.

1147
00:36:38,130 --> 00:36:40,327
-No, it's Shawn Phillips.

1148
00:36:40,327 --> 00:36:41,416
And Tim Robbins.

1149
00:36:41,416 --> 00:36:44,521
CHAD: Just been to church.

1150
00:36:44,521 --> 00:36:47,277
Your young friend
missed his vocation.

1151
00:36:47,277 --> 00:36:48,585
Should've been an evangelist.

1152
00:36:48,585 --> 00:36:49,763
-Too much like acting.

1153
00:36:49,763 --> 00:36:51,481
-He does have a way with words.

1154
00:36:51,481 --> 00:36:53,698
-Uh, not in the
right order, but--

1155
00:36:53,698 --> 00:36:54,736
CHAD: Or you.

1156
00:36:54,736 --> 00:36:56,094
You're leaving Clearwater.

1157
00:36:56,094 --> 00:36:59,449
-You're very nosy, kitty cat.

1158
00:36:59,449 --> 00:37:00,618
You know what we
do to nosy people?

1159
00:37:00,618 --> 00:37:04,003
-You're not going to get
out of this one, pal.

1160
00:37:04,003 --> 00:37:11,172
-Ho!

1161
00:37:11,172 --> 00:37:13,100
-Thank you,
crotch-rocketed friend.

1162
00:37:13,100 --> 00:37:14,308
-Don't forget to say [INAUDIBLE]

1163
00:37:18,122 --> 00:37:21,088
-OK, we'll just follow
you to the party, then.

1164
00:37:21,088 --> 00:37:22,945
-How long since you've
been on one of these?

1165
00:37:22,945 --> 00:37:24,383
-Long time.

1166
00:37:24,383 --> 00:37:25,332
Feels good.

1167
00:37:25,332 --> 00:37:26,900
-Must be the new seat covers.

1168
00:37:26,900 --> 00:37:28,427
They hit Mr. Bubble!
-Oh no.

1169
00:37:28,427 --> 00:37:29,686
-I don't know if
you're going to enjoy

1170
00:37:29,686 --> 00:37:30,814
the experience all that much.

1171
00:37:33,560 --> 00:37:35,966
-Lean into it.

1172
00:37:35,966 --> 00:37:37,454
-Hold on!

1173
00:37:37,454 --> 00:37:39,941
-Sunday, at Great Lakes Dragaway
in Union Grove, Wisconsin!

1174
00:37:42,926 --> 00:37:44,414
-Mighty Mikes, with
action traction.

1175
00:37:44,414 --> 00:37:47,899
-Lean into it.

1176
00:37:53,321 --> 00:37:54,460
-A garden weasel!

1177
00:37:54,460 --> 00:37:55,538
-Ooh.

1178
00:37:55,538 --> 00:37:56,846
Where did he have that?

1179
00:37:56,846 --> 00:37:58,743
-Hi-Keeba.
Huzzah.

1180
00:38:02,648 --> 00:38:03,896
-He's off the ramp.

1181
00:38:03,896 --> 00:38:04,815
-What ramp?

1182
00:38:04,815 --> 00:38:05,743
-Hair blowing in the wind.

1183
00:38:08,369 --> 00:38:09,208
My colon.

1184
00:38:09,208 --> 00:38:11,595
-Oh, I saw that in
a Mapplethorpe film.

1185
00:38:11,595 --> 00:38:14,970
-D'oh!

1186
00:38:14,970 --> 00:38:16,218
-Um, why don't they
just turn right?

1187
00:38:19,473 --> 00:38:21,141
-Oh, I'll harm you.

1188
00:38:21,141 --> 00:38:29,339
-Hey.

1189
00:38:29,339 --> 00:38:31,786
Hey, I just wanted to tell
you you got a tail light out.

1190
00:38:31,786 --> 00:38:34,082
Gee,

1191
00:38:34,082 --> 00:38:35,430
-He's got a licorice whip.

1192
00:38:35,430 --> 00:38:38,466
-Mmm.
-Yippee-yi-ay, tie-ay, cowboy!

1193
00:38:38,466 --> 00:38:40,932
-Tay.

1194
00:38:40,932 --> 00:38:42,540
-Where'd you learn that one?

1195
00:38:42,540 --> 00:38:43,519
-Motorcycle school.

1196
00:38:43,519 --> 00:38:44,507
-You're cute.

1197
00:38:55,331 --> 00:38:57,309
-That'll happen.

1198
00:38:57,309 --> 00:38:59,765
-Yeah.

1199
00:38:59,765 --> 00:39:00,754
-Where's Carrie?

1200
00:39:00,754 --> 00:39:02,321
-At a union meeting.

1201
00:39:02,321 --> 00:39:03,799
We'd better get to her tonight.

1202
00:39:03,799 --> 00:39:05,656
-It's potluck.
We've got to bring a dish.

1203
00:39:16,880 --> 00:39:18,018
-Lee?

1204
00:39:18,018 --> 00:39:20,095
Oh, Lee.
-Lee.

1205
00:39:20,095 --> 00:39:21,014
-Here, boy.

1206
00:39:21,014 --> 00:39:24,130
-Oh, couldn't you
stay in the van?

1207
00:39:24,130 --> 00:39:26,856
MAX (VOICEOVER): We were right.

1208
00:39:26,856 --> 00:39:27,754
-Carrie?

1209
00:39:27,754 --> 00:39:28,513
Carrie, it's Max.

1210
00:39:28,513 --> 00:39:29,302
-It's a medieval house.

1211
00:39:29,302 --> 00:39:33,536
Ooh.

1212
00:39:33,536 --> 00:39:34,764
-They varnished my house.

1213
00:39:34,764 --> 00:39:36,112
-You all right?

1214
00:39:36,112 --> 00:39:38,469
What's happened?

1215
00:39:38,469 --> 00:39:40,835
-I found it like this
when I came home.

1216
00:39:40,835 --> 00:39:43,881
-They disconnected cable and
hooked up my stereo all wrong.

1217
00:39:46,747 --> 00:39:48,544
-They've torn the place apart.

1218
00:39:48,544 --> 00:39:50,182
-Nice flat.

1219
00:39:50,182 --> 00:39:50,971
Apartment, I mean.

1220
00:39:50,971 --> 00:39:52,029
-It's going to be OK.

1221
00:39:52,029 --> 00:39:53,727
We won't let them get you.

1222
00:39:53,727 --> 00:39:54,975
-Hey look, Ignatz was here.

1223
00:39:54,975 --> 00:39:55,804
-Huh.

1224
00:39:55,804 --> 00:39:57,521
-Oh, Bil Keane
ransacked the apartment.

1225
00:39:57,521 --> 00:39:58,020
-Oh.

1226
00:39:58,020 --> 00:39:58,969
-Or Billy did it.

1227
00:39:58,969 --> 00:39:59,688
-Billy did it.

1228
00:39:59,688 --> 00:40:02,234
-I know they killed my brother.

1229
00:40:02,234 --> 00:40:05,290
And now they're after me.

1230
00:40:05,290 --> 00:40:07,706
-How about a little
sugar for grandpa?

1231
00:40:07,706 --> 00:40:09,654
-Nobody can harm you.

1232
00:40:09,654 --> 00:40:10,622
Leave it to us.

1233
00:40:10,622 --> 00:40:16,983
-Oh.

1234
00:40:16,983 --> 00:40:19,469
-Well, that's--
what do we do now?

1235
00:40:19,469 --> 00:40:20,658
-Big, brave girl.

1236
00:40:20,658 --> 00:40:21,546
-Well, brave, anyway.

1237
00:40:21,546 --> 00:40:24,213
-I stir everything up
and I can't handle it.

1238
00:40:24,213 --> 00:40:25,850
-They've warned you, OK?

1239
00:40:25,850 --> 00:40:27,058
Now it's up to us.

1240
00:40:27,058 --> 00:40:28,516
We're going to nail
Webster for good.

1241
00:40:28,516 --> 00:40:30,424
You just sit tight.

1242
00:40:30,424 --> 00:40:32,481
They won't be back.

1243
00:40:32,481 --> 00:40:33,689
But we will.

1244
00:40:33,689 --> 00:40:36,934
-We won't let the seniors
beat up on you anymore, Honey.

1245
00:40:36,934 --> 00:40:38,901
-Ooh.

1246
00:40:38,901 --> 00:40:40,679
-Ecch.

1247
00:40:40,679 --> 00:40:42,995
-You kiss better
than the union boss.

1248
00:40:42,995 --> 00:40:46,211
-Oh!

1249
00:40:46,211 --> 00:40:47,009
-Oh.

1250
00:40:47,009 --> 00:40:48,198
Dr. Pepper lip gloss.

1251
00:40:48,198 --> 00:40:51,473
Mm.
-Um.

1252
00:40:51,473 --> 00:40:52,761
-He's not getting back in.

1253
00:40:55,567 --> 00:40:58,732
-"Then Came Bronson,"
the van years.

1254
00:40:58,732 --> 00:41:00,220
MAX: We could go to the police.

1255
00:41:00,220 --> 00:41:01,828
-And tell them what?

1256
00:41:01,828 --> 00:41:02,617
-That Webster tried to

1257
00:41:02,617 --> 00:41:04,574
-[MOCKING NOISES]

1258
00:41:04,574 --> 00:41:05,073
-Hm?

1259
00:41:05,073 --> 00:41:06,132
-Then where's the proof?

1260
00:41:06,132 --> 00:41:08,199
Webster and all
those cannery workers

1261
00:41:08,199 --> 00:41:11,015
will have an alibi
for this afternoon.

1262
00:41:11,015 --> 00:41:13,122
-Gotta find something on him.

1263
00:41:13,122 --> 00:41:16,926
-If what Carrie says is true,
if they did kill her brother,

1264
00:41:16,926 --> 00:41:18,404
most people don't
cover their tracks

1265
00:41:18,404 --> 00:41:19,762
as well as they think they do.

1266
00:41:19,762 --> 00:41:21,739
-They should wear
long-sleeved shirts.

1267
00:41:21,739 --> 00:41:23,167
-Yeah.

1268
00:41:23,167 --> 00:41:24,615
-When?

1269
00:41:24,615 --> 00:41:26,133
-Tonight.
-Tonight?

1270
00:41:26,133 --> 00:41:30,157
Aw, I was going to
watch my dad's show.

1271
00:41:30,157 --> 00:41:31,904
-Glaucoma theater.

1272
00:41:31,904 --> 00:41:33,143
-Ooh.
Oh.

1273
00:41:33,143 --> 00:41:34,501
There.

1274
00:41:34,501 --> 00:41:35,409
-Yeah.

1275
00:41:35,409 --> 00:41:37,596
Looks like the set
for Graffiti Bridge.

1276
00:41:37,596 --> 00:41:40,652
-Oh.

1277
00:41:40,652 --> 00:41:42,878
Oh, I think it's a
Janet Jackson video.

1278
00:41:42,878 --> 00:41:43,897
Yeah, uh, "Rhythm Nation."

1279
00:41:43,897 --> 00:41:48,001
Mhm.
-[BEATBOXING]

1280
00:41:48,001 --> 00:41:51,216
-Uh, Lee, I-- I don't think
you're invisible, you know?

1281
00:41:51,216 --> 00:41:52,055
-Oh, swell.

1282
00:41:52,055 --> 00:42:02,380
-Huh?

1283
00:42:02,380 --> 00:42:04,936
Wait, he just climbed
into the basement.

1284
00:42:04,936 --> 00:42:07,163
What's going on.

1285
00:42:07,163 --> 00:42:09,450
-Hey.

1286
00:42:09,450 --> 00:42:12,306
-Ow! $4 an hour for this crap.
-Yeah.

1287
00:42:12,306 --> 00:42:13,694
-Too easy.

1288
00:42:13,694 --> 00:42:15,341
-Uh oh.

1289
00:42:15,341 --> 00:42:24,089
You would have to climb into
a security guard warehouse.

1290
00:42:24,089 --> 00:42:25,756
-Well, thanks for the help, Lee.

1291
00:42:25,756 --> 00:42:27,284
-I stopped for a Heath bar.

1292
00:42:27,284 --> 00:42:28,762
Want a bite?

1293
00:42:28,762 --> 00:42:30,380
-Hmm.

1294
00:42:30,380 --> 00:42:31,128
Let's see.

1295
00:42:31,128 --> 00:42:34,104
Where are we in the
script now, Tim?

1296
00:42:34,104 --> 00:42:36,021
-They're selling
fake term papers!

1297
00:42:36,021 --> 00:42:37,090
-D'oh!
-Found something.

1298
00:42:37,090 --> 00:42:38,438
Look at this.

1299
00:42:38,438 --> 00:42:39,037
Names.

1300
00:42:39,037 --> 00:42:41,444
Lists of names.

1301
00:42:41,444 --> 00:42:42,063
-Let's see here.

1302
00:42:42,063 --> 00:42:45,038
Bag of quicklime, three-foot
grave, red marker.

1303
00:42:45,038 --> 00:42:46,396
Murdered at Old Point!

1304
00:42:46,396 --> 00:42:47,984
-And that's the date
that he disappeared.

1305
00:42:47,984 --> 00:42:49,841
-Hm.

1306
00:42:49,841 --> 00:42:51,239
-Old Point.

1307
00:42:51,239 --> 00:42:52,168
Whatever that is.

1308
00:42:52,168 --> 00:42:53,107
-It's a beer, isn't it?

1309
00:42:53,107 --> 00:42:54,045
Or something?

1310
00:42:54,045 --> 00:42:55,723
-Coroner's involved, too.

1311
00:42:55,723 --> 00:42:57,560
-Time to go.

1312
00:42:57,560 --> 00:42:58,838
-Decided to protect himself.

1313
00:42:58,838 --> 00:43:00,196
-By hiding his emotions.

1314
00:43:00,196 --> 00:43:01,684
-You know, I'll bet
Webster doesn't even

1315
00:43:01,684 --> 00:43:04,570
know about these files.

1316
00:43:04,570 --> 00:43:13,058
-Let's get them over to
the Sheriff's office.

1317
00:43:13,058 --> 00:43:14,346
-Hey hold it steady there, Gyps.

1318
00:43:14,346 --> 00:43:28,535
Come on.

1319
00:43:28,535 --> 00:43:30,343
-Atten-hut!

1320
00:43:30,343 --> 00:43:33,718
General Van Patten!

1321
00:43:33,718 --> 00:43:35,964
-Be seated.

1322
00:43:35,964 --> 00:43:40,748
Now I want you to remember
that no "babbisterb" ever

1323
00:43:40,748 --> 00:43:44,442
"wub" a war by "dibbing"
for his "cubbity."

1324
00:43:44,442 --> 00:43:48,716
He won it by making the other
poor dumb "babbisterb" "dib"

1325
00:43:48,716 --> 00:43:50,174
"por" "ibs" "cubbity."

1326
00:43:50,174 --> 00:43:51,622
-Oh, we got Movie Sign!

1327
00:44:04,214 --> 00:44:06,810
-You whacked me with
your riding crop, Crow.

1328
00:44:06,810 --> 00:44:07,509
-Ah, you love it.

1329
00:44:07,509 --> 00:44:09,596
You know you do.

1330
00:44:09,596 --> 00:44:11,173
-Mister Webster?

1331
00:44:11,173 --> 00:44:12,392
Zalid Kerry.

1332
00:44:12,392 --> 00:44:13,460
We've had a break-in.

1333
00:44:13,460 --> 00:44:14,628
-Uh, they were
dressed as plumbers.

1334
00:44:14,628 --> 00:44:15,537
Nah, the young kid.

1335
00:44:15,537 --> 00:44:17,205
The one that's been
hanging around here.

1336
00:44:17,205 --> 00:44:18,443
-Van Patten something or other.

1337
00:44:18,443 --> 00:44:22,128
-Hey.

1338
00:44:25,113 --> 00:44:26,022
-Oh no, not this again.

1339
00:44:26,022 --> 00:44:29,916
I thought that Jeep blew up.
-Hi.

1340
00:44:29,916 --> 00:44:31,514
You guys going to Rob's party?

1341
00:44:31,514 --> 00:44:32,832
Follow us.

1342
00:44:32,832 --> 00:44:35,229
-Hey, this looks like the end
of "Easy Rider," doesn't it?

1343
00:44:35,229 --> 00:44:35,728
-Good.

1344
00:44:35,728 --> 00:44:37,166
-Why don't you get a haircut?

1345
00:44:37,166 --> 00:44:41,480
-Uh oh.

1346
00:44:44,845 --> 00:44:46,462
-McCloud, you've
wrecked three vans.

1347
00:44:46,462 --> 00:44:47,411
Good job.

1348
00:44:47,411 --> 00:44:49,748
-Hey, he didn't even
go through the window.

1349
00:44:49,748 --> 00:44:50,247
-Huh.

1350
00:44:50,247 --> 00:44:51,715
-I'm the guy with
a snake on my face.

1351
00:44:51,715 --> 00:44:56,717
-What's this?

1352
00:44:56,717 --> 00:44:58,515
The end of "Master Ninja"?

1353
00:44:58,515 --> 00:45:01,840
Tune in tomorrow, same ninja
time, same ninja station.

1354
00:45:01,840 --> 00:45:07,691
-Huh huh.

1355
00:45:07,691 --> 00:45:09,079
Let's dress him funny.

1356
00:45:09,079 --> 00:45:10,967
Oh, he already is.

1357
00:45:10,967 --> 00:45:15,131
Hey, Tim's making a dirt angel.

1358
00:45:15,131 --> 00:45:15,700
-I'm Max Keller.

1359
00:45:15,700 --> 00:45:17,497
I used to be able to walk.

1360
00:45:17,497 --> 00:45:20,982
-Is it curtains for Van Patten?

1361
00:45:20,982 --> 00:45:21,961
-He's dead.

1362
00:45:21,961 --> 00:45:22,800
-You've killed him.

1363
00:45:22,800 --> 00:45:23,588
Hail Dorothy.

1364
00:45:26,584 --> 00:45:30,069
-I could speak clearly
now, my brain is gone.

1365
00:45:30,069 --> 00:45:35,232
-Mendoza!

1366
00:45:39,655 --> 00:45:41,712
-Hey, Tim, don't
boot on the hood.

1367
00:45:41,712 --> 00:45:44,318
It'll take the wax off.

1368
00:45:44,318 --> 00:45:48,712
-Yes, it's the sporty Cutlass
Supreme, wide tracking Pontiac.

1369
00:45:48,712 --> 00:45:51,139
[COUGHING]

1370
00:45:51,139 --> 00:45:52,337
-My dad's going to kill me.

1371
00:45:55,732 --> 00:45:56,730
-Pull!

1372
00:45:56,730 --> 00:45:57,389
-Let him go.

1373
00:45:57,389 --> 00:45:58,718
Let him go.

1374
00:45:58,718 --> 00:46:00,575
-Yeah, nothing worse than
a cornered Van Patten.

1375
00:46:00,575 --> 00:46:01,703
-Mhm.
-They'll climb you.

1376
00:46:01,703 --> 00:46:03,630
-Pick him up later.

1377
00:46:03,630 --> 00:46:06,057
No place in this county he can
go that I won't hear about.

1378
00:46:06,057 --> 00:46:08,443
-Yeah, what about
Carrie's place?

1379
00:46:08,443 --> 00:46:09,302
-Maybe.

1380
00:46:09,302 --> 00:46:11,209
-Well, he'll come up empty.

1381
00:46:11,209 --> 00:46:13,955
Because the little
lady won't be there.

1382
00:46:13,955 --> 00:46:15,443
-Big hand for the little lady.

1383
00:46:15,443 --> 00:46:17,211
-First, put the body in the car.
-Sorry, boss.

1384
00:46:17,211 --> 00:46:19,048
My first caper.
-Take him to the Old Point.

1385
00:46:19,048 --> 00:46:19,727
Meet us there.

1386
00:46:19,727 --> 00:46:21,005
After you pick up the girl.

1387
00:46:21,005 --> 00:46:23,581
-Before you put your
coats on the top peg.

1388
00:46:23,581 --> 00:46:24,520
-Wait a minute.

1389
00:46:24,520 --> 00:46:26,168
-You said we'd
have dinner first.

1390
00:46:26,168 --> 00:46:26,667
-[BURP]

1391
00:46:26,667 --> 00:46:28,125
-Bad enough one man is dead.

1392
00:46:28,125 --> 00:46:29,483
You can't hurt that girl.

1393
00:46:29,483 --> 00:46:30,092
They'll find--

1394
00:46:30,092 --> 00:46:31,440
-They'll find nothing.

1395
00:46:31,440 --> 00:46:32,658
Ain't no bodies.

1396
00:46:32,658 --> 00:46:34,196
Two transients moved on.

1397
00:46:34,196 --> 00:46:35,704
-You're starting to
look delicious, boss.

1398
00:46:35,704 --> 00:46:37,431
-Headstrong girl that
couldn't take any longer.

1399
00:46:37,431 --> 00:46:39,478
She left town.

1400
00:46:39,478 --> 00:46:40,117
-No.

1401
00:46:40,117 --> 00:46:41,456
I won't be part of this.

1402
00:46:41,456 --> 00:46:43,433
-Carter, you've been a
part of it since the day

1403
00:46:43,433 --> 00:46:45,749
you decided that
comfortable job of yours

1404
00:46:45,749 --> 00:46:47,317
was more important that
telling the police what

1405
00:46:47,317 --> 00:46:48,455
happened to Carrie's brother.

1406
00:46:48,455 --> 00:46:49,124
-I'm sorry.

1407
00:46:49,124 --> 00:46:50,622
I was thinking about pork chops.

1408
00:46:50,622 --> 00:46:53,368
-Understand?

1409
00:46:53,368 --> 00:46:57,003
-I'm so hungry. [CRIES]
-Yes.

1410
00:46:57,003 --> 00:47:01,956
-Jeep.

1411
00:47:05,930 --> 00:47:07,158
-The Wizard's not in.

1412
00:47:07,158 --> 00:47:08,456
-And that's one.

1413
00:47:11,582 --> 00:47:12,610
-Come on, Max.

1414
00:47:12,610 --> 00:47:13,609
Four-minute mile.

1415
00:47:13,609 --> 00:47:14,777
Could stay the
course or beat them.

1416
00:47:14,777 --> 00:47:16,035
-[MOCKING NOISES]

1417
00:47:16,035 --> 00:47:18,402
-Could strain a course of bacon?

1418
00:47:18,402 --> 00:47:20,808
-No pain.

1419
00:47:20,808 --> 00:47:22,826
Outside yourself.
-What?

1420
00:47:22,826 --> 00:47:23,974
-When fighting go?

1421
00:47:23,974 --> 00:47:24,723
-What she said.

1422
00:47:24,723 --> 00:47:27,689
-My peach is dipped in sawd--
what-- what is he saying?

1423
00:47:31,443 --> 00:47:36,306
-The mediocrity of the
long-distance runner.

1424
00:47:36,306 --> 00:47:37,894
-Oh no, it's Jim Fixx!

1425
00:47:37,894 --> 00:47:38,822
Oh.
Sorry.

1426
00:47:38,822 --> 00:47:39,322
Bad joke.

1427
00:47:39,322 --> 00:47:41,259
-You know, his brother
was the bionic boy.

1428
00:47:41,259 --> 00:47:42,287
-Oh yeah.
That was cool.

1429
00:47:42,287 --> 00:47:45,293
-Carrie.

1430
00:47:45,293 --> 00:47:46,701
-Trick or treat for UNICEF.

1431
00:47:46,701 --> 00:47:48,259
-Max.

1432
00:47:48,259 --> 00:47:49,677
-Oh, you're sweaty.

1433
00:47:49,677 --> 00:47:50,416
A sweaty Van Patten.

1434
00:47:50,416 --> 00:47:51,993
-We've got to get out
of here right now.

1435
00:47:51,993 --> 00:47:53,801
Webster's on his way
over here with his pals.

1436
00:47:53,801 --> 00:47:55,618
I'm going to the police.

1437
00:47:55,618 --> 00:47:57,296
-Well, where's--

1438
00:47:57,296 --> 00:47:58,134
-Dead.

1439
00:47:58,134 --> 00:48:00,052
Come on!
-Wait.

1440
00:48:00,052 --> 00:48:03,926
Wait.
I need my lip gloss.

1441
00:48:03,926 --> 00:48:04,895
-Uh oh.

1442
00:48:04,895 --> 00:48:08,499
-Uh, you know how to
whistle, don't you?

1443
00:48:08,499 --> 00:48:09,318
-Get in the car.

1444
00:48:09,318 --> 00:48:10,676
-Oh, they pick up and deliver.

1445
00:48:10,676 --> 00:48:12,024
-Get in the car.

1446
00:48:12,024 --> 00:48:14,011
-Gee, thanks There's
some guys following us,

1447
00:48:14,011 --> 00:48:15,509
and it'd really be great if--oh.

1448
00:48:15,509 --> 00:48:16,997
Wait.

1449
00:48:16,997 --> 00:48:19,483
-Chic jeans

1450
00:48:19,483 --> 00:48:20,742
-OK, everybody in?

1451
00:48:20,742 --> 00:48:23,408
Good.

1452
00:48:23,408 --> 00:48:25,664
-The Steven King book club.

1453
00:48:25,664 --> 00:48:27,552
-How do you like
our little film?

1454
00:48:27,552 --> 00:48:29,719
Something seems a little fishy.

1455
00:48:29,719 --> 00:48:31,666
[CACKLES]

1456
00:48:31,666 --> 00:48:34,532
-Kill him.

1457
00:48:34,532 --> 00:48:36,269
-Are those Skylon Air Max?

1458
00:48:36,269 --> 00:48:38,626
I thought so.

1459
00:48:38,626 --> 00:48:42,230
-Charles Moffett feared not.

1460
00:48:42,230 --> 00:48:44,637
Puma?

1461
00:48:44,637 --> 00:48:45,416
-Alas, poor Yorick.

1462
00:48:45,416 --> 00:48:47,952
I knew him.

1463
00:48:47,952 --> 00:48:50,768
-Hey, you plant your
ninjas in the late fall.

1464
00:48:50,768 --> 00:48:51,267
-Huh.

1465
00:48:51,267 --> 00:48:52,036
-I don't know.

1466
00:48:52,036 --> 00:48:53,754
Three, 3 1/2 feet.

1467
00:48:53,754 --> 00:48:56,450
Throw him in.

1468
00:48:56,450 --> 00:48:59,196
-Oh, they're doing
"Blood Simple."

1469
00:48:59,196 --> 00:49:00,204
-Webster, you--

1470
00:49:00,204 --> 00:49:00,903
-Thesaurus!

1471
00:49:00,903 --> 00:49:02,900
-Patience, pal.

1472
00:49:02,900 --> 00:49:05,137
Your moment's coming.

1473
00:49:05,137 --> 00:49:06,735
And hers.
-Hm.

1474
00:49:06,735 --> 00:49:07,234
-Yeah.

1475
00:49:07,234 --> 00:49:08,992
Like you did for my brother?

1476
00:49:08,992 --> 00:49:09,990
-Mhm.
Pretty much.

1477
00:49:09,990 --> 00:49:11,228
-Two more, just like this one.

1478
00:49:11,228 --> 00:49:12,916
Unmarked.
[COUGHING]

1479
00:49:12,916 --> 00:49:14,594
-Hey, careful with that.

1480
00:49:14,594 --> 00:49:16,291
-They don't use it anymore.

1481
00:49:16,291 --> 00:49:17,529
No caretaker, no maintenance.

1482
00:49:17,529 --> 00:49:18,678
-No tears and no
hearts breaking.

1483
00:49:18,678 --> 00:49:19,976
No remorse.

1484
00:49:19,976 --> 00:49:22,183
-Now I got it, Webster.

1485
00:49:22,183 --> 00:49:23,341
It's been really bothering me.

1486
00:49:23,341 --> 00:49:24,769
-It was you behind
the dictionary capers.

1487
00:49:24,769 --> 00:49:25,268
-Ah!

1488
00:49:25,268 --> 00:49:27,595
-It's not just that you run
the cannery the way you want.

1489
00:49:27,595 --> 00:49:29,682
No flak, no union.

1490
00:49:29,682 --> 00:49:30,181
No.

1491
00:49:30,181 --> 00:49:31,299
-No tears, no--

1492
00:49:31,299 --> 00:49:33,117
-That's not enough
kill people for.

1493
00:49:33,117 --> 00:49:35,693
There's another reason.

1494
00:49:35,693 --> 00:49:37,011
-What would that be?

1495
00:49:37,011 --> 00:49:38,569
-You get off on it.

1496
00:49:38,569 --> 00:49:39,627
It's a thrill, a kick.

1497
00:49:39,627 --> 00:49:40,626
-Hm.
-Kicks!

1498
00:49:40,626 --> 00:49:42,263
-The power you have.

1499
00:49:42,263 --> 00:49:42,912
Life and death.

1500
00:49:42,912 --> 00:49:43,981
[COUGHING]

1501
00:49:43,981 --> 00:49:45,668
-It's a tough act to top.

1502
00:49:45,668 --> 00:49:47,086
-What I feel and
what I think isn't

1503
00:49:47,086 --> 00:49:49,982
going to matter to you
much longer, Keller.

1504
00:49:49,982 --> 00:49:50,891
Any more than it does--

1505
00:49:50,891 --> 00:49:51,390
-Hey, hey!

1506
00:49:51,390 --> 00:49:54,166
More dirt in the hole,
less on my shoes.

1507
00:49:54,166 --> 00:49:56,213
-Or you, Carter,

1508
00:49:56,213 --> 00:49:57,242
-Me?

1509
00:49:57,242 --> 00:49:58,630
-Didn't I tell you, Carter?

1510
00:49:58,630 --> 00:50:00,427
After 35 years of
keeping your nose clean

1511
00:50:00,427 --> 00:50:04,691
and looking the other
way, this is what you get.

1512
00:50:04,691 --> 00:50:07,607
-I'm not dead yet.
-Uh oh.

1513
00:50:11,910 --> 00:50:13,089
-Dirt man.

1514
00:50:13,089 --> 00:50:14,077
In color.

1515
00:50:14,077 --> 00:50:17,602
-I hope he doesn't
soil his ninja robes.

1516
00:50:17,602 --> 00:50:19,959
[CACKLING]

1517
00:50:19,959 --> 00:50:21,956
-Terrible.

1518
00:50:21,956 --> 00:50:22,865
Ooh.

1519
00:50:22,865 --> 00:50:24,322
Have you learned
the plot? [CACKLING]

1520
00:50:24,322 --> 00:50:25,611
-Stop it.
-Enjoy.

1521
00:50:25,611 --> 00:50:26,579
-Stop it.

1522
00:50:26,579 --> 00:50:28,017
-I feel so dirty.

1523
00:50:28,017 --> 00:50:32,121
-I'll just be by the
tree, looking cute.

1524
00:50:32,121 --> 00:50:35,087
-Oof.
Oof.

1525
00:50:35,087 --> 00:50:39,031
-That's the sound
of cardboard boxes.

1526
00:50:39,031 --> 00:50:40,259
-Oh no, that's
the Rosetta stone.

1527
00:50:40,259 --> 00:50:41,657
Don't, it's-- Oh!

1528
00:50:41,657 --> 00:50:45,312
-Rock, paper, scissor.
-Thanks.

1529
00:50:48,727 --> 00:50:50,375
-Uh, I was just reaching
for the Snickers bar

1530
00:50:50,375 --> 00:50:52,571
in the glove box, boss.

1531
00:50:52,571 --> 00:50:54,688
-Hey, Butterball,
that's for Dom DeLuise.

1532
00:50:59,112 --> 00:51:00,989
-He's got trouble in spades.

1533
00:51:00,989 --> 00:51:03,855
-Yeah.
-Ho ho!

1534
00:51:03,855 --> 00:51:07,180
-Don't forget to have
your ninja spayed.

1535
00:51:07,180 --> 00:51:08,269
-Hey, he's Little John.

1536
00:51:16,027 --> 00:51:18,813
-Well, are you digging
this theme? [CACKLING]

1537
00:51:18,813 --> 00:51:20,830
-Stop him.
Stop him.

1538
00:51:20,830 --> 00:51:22,139
-Thank you.

1539
00:51:22,139 --> 00:51:24,036
-Ooh, right in the keister.

1540
00:51:24,036 --> 00:51:26,962
-Kunta Kinte.

1541
00:51:26,962 --> 00:51:28,429
-Hit him It's dark.

1542
00:51:28,429 --> 00:51:30,137
-No one will know.

1543
00:51:30,137 --> 00:51:31,205
-I know.

1544
00:51:31,205 --> 00:51:33,512
Lowered heartbeat
to barely a pulse.

1545
00:51:33,512 --> 00:51:35,539
Close your door to the senses
like you did in the motel room.

1546
00:51:35,539 --> 00:51:37,237
-No, I was really dead, Tim.

1547
00:51:37,237 --> 00:51:39,673
I'm the Reanimator.

1548
00:51:39,673 --> 00:51:41,241
-On the edge of death.

1549
00:51:41,241 --> 00:51:44,696
It can only be
effective once or twice.

1550
00:51:44,696 --> 00:51:45,275
More than that--

1551
00:51:45,275 --> 00:51:47,512
-It loses its impact.

1552
00:51:47,512 --> 00:51:48,550
-Could prove fatal.

1553
00:51:48,550 --> 00:51:49,199
-Do it again.

1554
00:51:49,199 --> 00:51:49,948
I dare you.

1555
00:51:49,948 --> 00:51:51,766
-I'm glad you're alive.

1556
00:51:51,766 --> 00:51:52,854
-I prefer it.

1557
00:51:52,854 --> 00:51:54,482
-We are good friends, Tim.

1558
00:51:58,606 --> 00:52:00,912
-Epilogue, where you always
get to see Lee's gut.

1559
00:52:00,912 --> 00:52:01,941
Yep.
And there it is.

1560
00:52:01,941 --> 00:52:03,459
You see what I mean?

1561
00:52:03,459 --> 00:52:04,527
-When does it happen.

1562
00:52:04,527 --> 00:52:05,805
-Oh, about in a week.

1563
00:52:05,805 --> 00:52:07,373
But listen.

1564
00:52:07,373 --> 00:52:09,520
I want to thank
y'all for proving

1565
00:52:09,520 --> 00:52:11,617
something that I believed in.

1566
00:52:11,617 --> 00:52:12,575
-You cannot act.

1567
00:52:12,575 --> 00:52:13,854
-Sorry about your
brother, Carrie.

1568
00:52:13,854 --> 00:52:16,290
-Well, at least I
know for sure, now.

1569
00:52:16,290 --> 00:52:17,079
I needed to.

1570
00:52:17,079 --> 00:52:17,798
-Sob, sob.

1571
00:52:17,798 --> 00:52:18,567
Remorse, remorse.

1572
00:52:18,567 --> 00:52:19,465
Well!

1573
00:52:19,465 --> 00:52:24,159
-If you still need a job, I
can get you one with the union.

1574
00:52:24,159 --> 00:52:25,986
-I'll be in the van with Henry.

1575
00:52:25,986 --> 00:52:30,000
-Reading "Of Mice and Men."

1576
00:52:30,000 --> 00:52:30,549
-A classy guy.

1577
00:52:30,549 --> 00:52:31,328
-He's an idiot.

1578
00:52:31,328 --> 00:52:34,983
-I still don't know how you made
those people think he was dead.

1579
00:52:34,983 --> 00:52:37,529
-It's a long story, going back--

1580
00:52:37,529 --> 00:52:40,185
-Oh, an hour.

1581
00:52:40,185 --> 00:52:41,494
-Listen, about that job.

1582
00:52:41,494 --> 00:52:42,382
-You're fired.

1583
00:52:42,382 --> 00:52:44,090
-Heh heh heh heh.

1584
00:52:44,090 --> 00:52:45,228
-I've got to go, Carrie.

1585
00:52:45,228 --> 00:52:45,947
-No.

1586
00:52:45,947 --> 00:52:46,696
-Oh.

1587
00:52:46,696 --> 00:52:49,422
-There's someone we have
to find for the Master.

1588
00:52:49,422 --> 00:52:51,369
-The Master?
-My friend.

1589
00:52:51,369 --> 00:52:53,316
It's kind of a nickname.

1590
00:52:53,316 --> 00:52:55,783
Hey, I'll be back this
way for next year's race.

1591
00:52:55,783 --> 00:52:56,672
-You better be.

1592
00:52:56,672 --> 00:52:58,399
-Because I'll be
of legal age then.

1593
00:52:58,399 --> 00:53:00,556
-Hey.
-Sorry.

1594
00:53:00,556 --> 00:53:02,693
-Oh, she tastes like salmon.

1595
00:53:02,693 --> 00:53:03,661
-Ooh.

1596
00:53:03,661 --> 00:53:06,078
-Hey.

1597
00:53:06,078 --> 00:53:08,015
-What are we in the middle of?
-What's this?

1598
00:53:08,015 --> 00:53:08,984
-Take my hand.

1599
00:53:08,984 --> 00:53:10,841
-I'm a stranger in paradise.

1600
00:53:10,841 --> 00:53:13,777
-But there's nothing
wrong with my car!

1601
00:53:13,777 --> 00:53:15,185
-Now!
Pull up.

1602
00:53:15,185 --> 00:53:16,583
ALICIA: I can't do it.

1603
00:53:16,583 --> 00:53:17,921
-Grab hold of the wire.

1604
00:53:17,921 --> 00:53:20,167
-We missed something.

1605
00:53:20,167 --> 00:53:21,665
-Who's that?

1606
00:53:21,665 --> 00:53:26,638
-[MOCKING NOISES]

1607
00:53:26,638 --> 00:53:27,627
-OK.

1608
00:53:27,627 --> 00:53:29,095
See if you can shake
her loose now, Tim.

1609
00:53:29,095 --> 00:53:33,458
Hoo.

1610
00:53:33,458 --> 00:53:38,780
-Next week, on "Mannix."

1611
00:53:38,780 --> 00:53:41,087
-Hey look, Bud Cort's up
at the top of the cliff,

1612
00:53:41,087 --> 00:53:41,946
playing a banjo.

1613
00:53:41,946 --> 00:53:43,234
Heh.

1614
00:53:43,234 --> 00:53:44,342
-Matt Houston.

1615
00:53:44,342 --> 00:53:44,991
In color.

1616
00:53:48,526 --> 00:53:51,732
-(SINGING) Those magnificent
men in their flying machines.

1617
00:53:51,732 --> 00:53:52,630
-All right.

1618
00:53:52,630 --> 00:53:54,408
See that guy down there?
-Jump on him.

1619
00:53:54,408 --> 00:53:56,185
-I'm going to drop
you off right by him.

1620
00:53:56,185 --> 00:53:56,994
-No, I--
-Ew.

1621
00:53:56,994 --> 00:53:58,382
Ew.
I hate him.

1622
00:53:58,382 --> 00:53:58,422
Ew.
I hate him.
Ew.

1623
00:53:58,422 --> 00:53:58,881
Ew.

1624
00:53:58,881 --> 00:53:59,800
-It's going to be OK.

1625
00:53:59,800 --> 00:54:01,657
When I tell you
to drop, you drop.

1626
00:54:01,657 --> 00:54:03,445
-What in the Sam H-- oh.

1627
00:54:03,445 --> 00:54:06,830
MAX (ON RADIO): Look,

1628
00:54:06,830 --> 00:54:09,236
going to drop her right by you.
So be ready.

1629
00:54:09,236 --> 00:54:11,553
-Timothy Van Patten
is "Sky King."

1630
00:54:11,553 --> 00:54:12,821
-Brought to you by Nabisco.

1631
00:54:12,821 --> 00:54:13,810
-Coming in.

1632
00:54:19,252 --> 00:54:20,730
Drop!

1633
00:54:20,730 --> 00:54:23,206
-Well, she is kind of
a blonde bombshell.

1634
00:54:23,206 --> 00:54:25,183
It's kind of a stretch.

1635
00:54:25,183 --> 00:54:28,149
-Ouch.

1636
00:54:28,149 --> 00:54:31,035
-And in an unrelated incident,
a car pulled over today.

1637
00:54:31,035 --> 00:54:33,781
More later.

1638
00:54:33,781 --> 00:54:36,696
-More better.

1639
00:54:36,696 --> 00:54:38,204
-Enjoy Red Wing shoes.

1640
00:54:40,870 --> 00:54:41,699
-I like to watch.

1641
00:54:44,515 --> 00:54:46,053
-Hey, we lost the sound.
-No, no.

1642
00:54:46,053 --> 00:54:46,552
It's--
-Oh, I--

1643
00:54:46,552 --> 00:54:47,251
It's--
-Oh, I--
-Oh, I--
-Yeah.

1644
00:54:47,251 --> 00:54:47,730
-Oh, I--
-Yeah.

1645
00:54:50,616 --> 00:54:52,064
-Hmm.

1646
00:54:52,064 --> 00:54:53,163
-You OK?

1647
00:54:53,163 --> 00:54:54,491
-I'm fine.

1648
00:54:54,491 --> 00:54:55,829
I'm fine.
-Yeah.

1649
00:54:55,829 --> 00:54:58,006
-I knew those brakes were gone.
I should have had them fixed.

1650
00:54:58,006 --> 00:54:58,804
There's just no time.

1651
00:54:58,804 --> 00:55:00,542
I mean, everything's
always too fast.

1652
00:55:00,542 --> 00:55:01,660
My whole life's always been--

1653
00:55:01,660 --> 00:55:02,629
-I should have dropped you.

1654
00:55:02,629 --> 00:55:04,097
-I mean, that last
bend back there,

1655
00:55:04,097 --> 00:55:07,222
I didn't even-- I didn't even
think I was going to make it.

1656
00:55:07,222 --> 00:55:07,722
-Slow down.

1657
00:55:07,722 --> 00:55:08,411
Take a breath.

1658
00:55:08,411 --> 00:55:09,858
You're going faster
than the car.

1659
00:55:09,858 --> 00:55:10,358
-I will.

1660
00:55:10,358 --> 00:55:12,055
I always talk too
fast, especially when

1661
00:55:12,055 --> 00:55:13,952
I'm scared half to death.

1662
00:55:13,952 --> 00:55:15,910
Who are you?

1663
00:55:15,910 --> 00:55:17,128
-Max Keller.

1664
00:55:17,128 --> 00:55:18,226
John McAllister.

1665
00:55:18,226 --> 00:55:20,773
-Oh, I'm Alicia Clayton,
native of California, freckles,

1666
00:55:20,773 --> 00:55:22,810
surfboards, and, uh, fast cars.

1667
00:55:22,810 --> 00:55:24,218
-What is this,
"The Dating Game"?

1668
00:55:24,218 --> 00:55:25,795
-[HUMS "SPANISH FLEA"]

1669
00:55:25,795 --> 00:55:27,703
-We'll take you home.
-Uh, wait a minute.

1670
00:55:27,703 --> 00:55:28,621
You saved my life.

1671
00:55:28,621 --> 00:55:29,859
And what am I supposed to do?

1672
00:55:29,859 --> 00:55:31,197
What am I supposed
to give you for that?

1673
00:55:31,197 --> 00:55:31,757
-Oh, just--

1674
00:55:31,757 --> 00:55:32,655
-A dollar?

1675
00:55:32,655 --> 00:55:33,684
-A dollar.
Good.

1676
00:55:33,684 --> 00:55:34,752
A big dollar.

1677
00:55:38,207 --> 00:55:39,196
-I have an idea.

1678
00:55:39,196 --> 00:55:39,785
-Oh, yes.

1679
00:55:39,785 --> 00:55:41,682
-There's a big garden party
this afternoon for family

1680
00:55:41,682 --> 00:55:42,841
and friends of the
European Summit.

1681
00:55:42,841 --> 00:55:43,749
Will you come?

1682
00:55:43,749 --> 00:55:45,447
-I don't think we'd
be on the guest list.

1683
00:55:45,447 --> 00:55:47,394
-Uh, we hate Europe.
-My father's Senator Clayton.

1684
00:55:47,394 --> 00:55:48,492
He's organizing the party.

1685
00:55:48,492 --> 00:55:49,401
-Senator Claghorn?

1686
00:55:49,401 --> 00:55:50,709
-Um, uh, do me a favor.

1687
00:55:50,709 --> 00:55:52,547
Don't say anything about
the sports car, OK?

1688
00:55:52,547 --> 00:55:54,114
I mean, he's had to put
up with added security,

1689
00:55:54,114 --> 00:55:55,203
bomb threats, secret
service agents.

1690
00:55:55,203 --> 00:55:56,361
All he needs now
is to hear about

1691
00:55:56,361 --> 00:55:57,989
a reckless-driving daughter.

1692
00:55:57,989 --> 00:55:58,598
4 o'clock.

1693
00:55:58,598 --> 00:56:00,315
Oh, and, uh, black tie.

1694
00:56:00,315 --> 00:56:01,234
OK?
-OK.

1695
00:56:01,234 --> 00:56:04,499
-OK.

1696
00:56:04,499 --> 00:56:05,877
-Get in.

1697
00:56:05,877 --> 00:56:08,194
-Well, bye.

1698
00:56:08,194 --> 00:56:09,322
-D'oh.

1699
00:56:09,322 --> 00:56:12,178
-Hey, shoot some of
this action my way.

1700
00:56:12,178 --> 00:56:13,636
-Maybe I should have kissed her.

1701
00:56:13,636 --> 00:56:15,264
-Would have been a good move.

1702
00:56:15,264 --> 00:56:17,640
-Garden party?

1703
00:56:17,640 --> 00:56:19,058
-We'll take it.

1704
00:56:19,058 --> 00:56:19,567
-Right.

1705
00:56:19,567 --> 00:56:21,135
-Do you think Rick
Nelson will be there?

1706
00:56:21,135 --> 00:56:22,134
-It's possible.

1707
00:56:22,134 --> 00:56:23,132
-Maybe.

1708
00:56:23,132 --> 00:56:24,630
Reminiscing.

1709
00:56:24,630 --> 00:56:26,737
-Eastern thugs go with flow.

1710
00:56:29,493 --> 00:56:30,242
-Ah, what a cliche.

1711
00:56:30,242 --> 00:56:32,439
A Japanese guy in a GM car.

1712
00:56:32,439 --> 00:56:35,364
-Xanadu, stately home
of Charles Foster Kane.

1713
00:56:35,364 --> 00:56:37,092
Cost?
No one can say.

1714
00:56:40,866 --> 00:56:43,722
-Looks like a Paul
Klee painting.

1715
00:56:43,722 --> 00:56:44,511
-Yeah.

1716
00:56:44,511 --> 00:56:46,179
CASTILE: We'll come from
the west, out of the sun.

1717
00:56:46,179 --> 00:56:48,006
-He's got a crown on.

1718
00:56:48,006 --> 00:56:48,505
CASTILE: Here.

1719
00:56:48,505 --> 00:56:50,103
SERENA: Senator Clayton
won't know what hit him.

1720
00:56:50,103 --> 00:56:50,632
We'll be ready.

1721
00:56:50,632 --> 00:56:53,957
Every man knows what
he's supposed to do.

1722
00:56:53,957 --> 00:56:55,525
How many people do
you want killed?

1723
00:56:55,525 --> 00:56:56,624
-How many you up for?

1724
00:56:56,624 --> 00:56:58,251
-No one.

1725
00:56:58,251 --> 00:57:00,388
We'll take what we
came for and out.

1726
00:57:00,388 --> 00:57:02,964
-Oh, the military's
wearing Zubaz now.

1727
00:57:02,964 --> 00:57:03,743
-Mm.

1728
00:57:03,743 --> 00:57:04,532
Neat.

1729
00:57:04,532 --> 00:57:05,461
I like.

1730
00:57:05,461 --> 00:57:07,857
-Not Sybil Danning, but
an incredible simulation.

1731
00:57:07,857 --> 00:57:10,633
-I'll say.

1732
00:57:10,633 --> 00:57:14,727
-Your loyalties must
be with the sect.

1733
00:57:14,727 --> 00:57:15,696
-Say "Uncle."

1734
00:57:15,696 --> 00:57:16,605
-David McCallum.

1735
00:57:16,605 --> 00:57:18,252
-My loyalties are to myself.

1736
00:57:18,252 --> 00:57:19,371
You know that.

1737
00:57:19,371 --> 00:57:21,118
-I can remember--

1738
00:57:21,118 --> 00:57:23,055
-Don't remember.

1739
00:57:23,055 --> 00:57:25,452
Memories are dangerous
in the wrong hands.

1740
00:57:25,452 --> 00:57:27,529
-Remember when you used
to get good work, Dave?

1741
00:57:27,529 --> 00:57:28,098
-Castile.

1742
00:57:28,098 --> 00:57:30,654
-Serena.

1743
00:57:30,654 --> 00:57:34,788
You want liberation for
your people, to free them,

1744
00:57:34,788 --> 00:57:36,486
What makes you think
this is the right way?

1745
00:57:36,486 --> 00:57:37,424
-Liberate this, David.

1746
00:57:37,424 --> 00:57:39,152
-If they don't heed
us, we'll kill them.

1747
00:57:39,152 --> 00:57:39,651
-Yeah.

1748
00:57:39,651 --> 00:57:41,848
Yeah, you'll kill anyone
who standing in your way,

1749
00:57:41,848 --> 00:57:43,306
for the cause.

1750
00:57:43,306 --> 00:57:45,882
That's the burden you carry.

1751
00:57:45,882 --> 00:57:48,009
I stopped believing in causes.

1752
00:57:48,009 --> 00:57:50,525
-Now I just believe in effects.

1753
00:57:50,525 --> 00:57:53,042
-For which we pay you
a great deal of money.

1754
00:57:53,042 --> 00:57:55,958
-Plus Dental and a 401(k).

1755
00:57:55,958 --> 00:57:58,154
-I'll get you what you need.
-Peroxide.

1756
00:57:58,154 --> 00:57:59,023
Lots of it.

1757
00:57:59,023 --> 00:58:03,856
-It might even work, If you
do exactly as you're told.

1758
00:58:03,856 --> 00:58:05,244
-She's Skeletor's sister.

1759
00:58:05,244 --> 00:58:06,173
-Look at her.

1760
00:58:06,173 --> 00:58:07,051
-Shelly-tor.

1761
00:58:07,051 --> 00:58:09,598
-What memories will
hold you back then?

1762
00:58:09,598 --> 00:58:10,926
-Well,we could sell
their plasma, maybe.

1763
00:58:10,926 --> 00:58:16,378
-- --won't cooperate,
you can kill them.

1764
00:58:16,378 --> 00:58:17,956
-Get Noel Harrison to do it.

1765
00:58:17,956 --> 00:58:19,374
-Ba-boom.
-Yeah.

1766
00:58:19,374 --> 00:58:23,468
MAX (VOICEOVER): It was a

1767
00:58:23,468 --> 00:58:25,035
And I figured the worst
that could happen to us

1768
00:58:25,035 --> 00:58:26,933
was eating a bad hors
d'oeuvres or drinking

1769
00:58:26,933 --> 00:58:28,620
too much champagne, right?

1770
00:58:28,620 --> 00:58:29,509
-Right.

1771
00:58:29,509 --> 00:58:31,137
MAX (VOICEOVER): Not when
you're with the Master.

1772
00:58:31,137 --> 00:58:32,874
-So, more "horse dee ovaries"?

1773
00:58:32,874 --> 00:58:33,753
There you go.

1774
00:58:33,753 --> 00:58:35,191
-David Eisenhower.

1775
00:58:35,191 --> 00:58:36,369
-Hi there.

1776
00:58:36,369 --> 00:58:36,868
Hi.

1777
00:58:36,868 --> 00:58:37,487
How's it going.

1778
00:58:37,487 --> 00:58:37,987
Hi.

1779
00:58:37,987 --> 00:58:38,815
-Hello, there.

1780
00:58:38,815 --> 00:58:43,479
-Hello.

1781
00:58:43,479 --> 00:58:45,666
-Who's winning the Dodger game?

1782
00:58:45,666 --> 00:58:48,491
Oh.
-Hi, Amy.

1783
00:58:48,491 --> 00:58:49,540
-Alicia, I need to ask you--

1784
00:58:49,540 --> 00:58:50,868
-Let me talk to you
in a little bit, OK?

1785
00:58:50,868 --> 00:58:52,386
-Kinda looks like Stevie
Nicks in that outfit.

1786
00:58:52,386 --> 00:58:53,095
-Yeah, I wouldn't
have recognized her

1787
00:58:53,095 --> 00:58:54,633
without a big bottle
of Wild Turkey, though.

1788
00:58:54,633 --> 00:58:58,557
-D'oh!

1789
00:58:58,557 --> 00:59:01,303
-Oh, left at the altar, huh?

1790
00:59:01,303 --> 00:59:03,180
-Ah.

1791
00:59:03,180 --> 00:59:05,077
-Good crowd, huh?
-Thank you.

1792
00:59:05,077 --> 00:59:08,113
-Told the Senator there'd
be a large attendance.

1793
00:59:08,113 --> 00:59:10,080
We could have our
own summit meeting.

1794
00:59:10,080 --> 00:59:12,337
-Move your hand or
lose it at the wrist.

1795
00:59:12,337 --> 00:59:12,836
-Ooh.

1796
00:59:12,836 --> 00:59:15,372
Such a smart mouth for
such a lovely lady.

1797
00:59:15,372 --> 00:59:16,451
-Get lost, Palin.

1798
00:59:16,451 --> 00:59:17,260
- --tologist.

1799
00:59:17,260 --> 00:59:18,797
-When are you going to
give me a break, huh?

1800
00:59:22,093 --> 00:59:24,040
-Greasy kid stuff.

1801
00:59:24,040 --> 00:59:25,188
-Oily.

1802
00:59:25,188 --> 00:59:25,907
Even in a tux.

1803
00:59:25,907 --> 00:59:28,234
How do you do it?
SENATOR CLAYTON: Johnny.

1804
00:59:28,234 --> 00:59:28,893
-Be good.

1805
00:59:28,893 --> 00:59:30,760
-Did you double the security?

1806
00:59:30,760 --> 00:59:31,749
-Everything's taken care of--

1807
00:59:31,749 --> 00:59:33,326
-Everybody's got two guns, sir.

