2839
01:33:36,294 --> 01:33:38,042
-Lee Van Cleef didn't
wear a pirate outfit

2840
01:33:38,042 --> 01:33:39,669
in this or any other film.

2841
01:33:39,669 --> 01:33:40,328
-I know.

2842
01:33:40,328 --> 01:33:41,816
It's just a fantasy
I've always had.

2843
01:33:41,816 --> 01:33:42,316
-[SIGH]

2844
01:33:42,316 --> 01:33:43,953
-Anyway, it's, uh,
time to read a letter.

2845
01:33:43,953 --> 01:33:44,452
-Oh!

2846
01:33:44,452 --> 01:33:46,619
-Let's put that up on
still store there, Cambot.

2847
01:33:46,619 --> 01:33:48,427
This one comes
from Sue Schroeder

2848
01:33:48,427 --> 01:33:49,974
of Rochester, New York.
-Hi, Sue.

2849
01:33:49,974 --> 01:33:51,692
-And Sue writes,
"I can certainly

2850
01:33:51,692 --> 01:33:53,989
sympathize with being
forced to watch bad movies,

2851
01:33:53,989 --> 01:33:56,815
because I am a Film
Studies grad student.

2852
01:33:56,815 --> 01:33:58,292
Most of the movies
that I have to watch,

2853
01:33:58,292 --> 01:34:01,298
though, are 10 hours
long, and have subtitles.

2854
01:34:01,298 --> 01:34:03,435
Actually, when I was
still taking classes,

2855
01:34:03,435 --> 01:34:04,973
I didn't even get to see movies.

2856
01:34:04,973 --> 01:34:07,359
All I did was read Freud
and dumb French people

2857
01:34:07,359 --> 01:34:09,027
like," uh, what's that word?

2858
01:34:09,027 --> 01:34:09,985
-Derrida.

2859
01:34:09,985 --> 01:34:10,565
-"Derrida."

2860
01:34:10,565 --> 01:34:11,653
-Foucault!

2861
01:34:11,653 --> 01:34:12,781
-"I hated it.

2862
01:34:12,781 --> 01:34:16,037
Well, I almost have my degree,
so it will all be over soon.

2863
01:34:16,037 --> 01:34:18,094
PS, I want to draw a
picture for you all,

2864
01:34:18,094 --> 01:34:20,221
but I have to get back
to writing my thesis.

2865
01:34:20,221 --> 01:34:22,847
Also, will you three say
what your favorite movies

2866
01:34:22,847 --> 01:34:23,526
of all time are?"

2867
01:34:23,526 --> 01:34:25,213
That's a good one.
-Well, for me it's easy.

2868
01:34:25,213 --> 01:34:26,751
"Big Fauss and Little Halsey."

2869
01:34:26,751 --> 01:34:27,400
-Crow?

2870
01:34:27,400 --> 01:34:29,407
-Kafka's "The Castle"
-I thought it was Zardoz.

2871
01:34:29,407 --> 01:34:32,473
-I'd have to say "Colossus:
the Forbin Project."

2872
01:34:32,473 --> 01:34:32,972
-Huh.

2873
01:34:32,972 --> 01:34:34,330
-What do you think, sirs?

2874
01:34:34,330 --> 01:34:36,008
-Ah, "The Forbin Project."

2875
01:34:36,008 --> 01:34:37,436
One of my favorite films.

2876
01:34:37,436 --> 01:34:38,794
The thing I like most about it--

2877
01:34:38,794 --> 01:34:40,861
-Doctor, can I
just say something?

2878
01:34:40,861 --> 01:34:42,039
Can I just get serious?

2879
01:34:42,039 --> 01:34:44,705
I have something
very important, which

2880
01:34:44,705 --> 01:34:46,113
I really feel has to be said.

2881
01:34:46,113 --> 01:34:47,751
-Well, Frank, you
do look serious.

2882
01:34:47,751 --> 01:34:50,157
Um, by all means,
the floor is yours.

2883
01:34:50,157 --> 01:34:50,876
-Thank you, doctor.

2884
01:34:50,876 --> 01:34:53,163
You know, one thing
in today's experiment

2885
01:34:53,163 --> 01:34:56,608
caused great pain to
yours truly, TVs Frank.

2886
01:34:56,608 --> 01:35:00,273
It was the appearance of Monte
Markham in the second episode--

2887
01:35:00,273 --> 01:35:02,719
I mean the later half--
of today's movie.

2888
01:35:02,719 --> 01:35:07,033
Monty Markham was the star of
my favorite television series

2889
01:35:07,033 --> 01:35:10,028
when I was just a young man,
living in my mother's basement.

2890
01:35:10,028 --> 01:35:13,933
I'm talking, of course, of "The
Second Hundred Years," which,

2891
01:35:13,933 --> 01:35:18,227
as you all remember, was the
crazy, wild show about him,

2892
01:35:18,227 --> 01:35:21,412
being trapped in suspended
animation for 100 years,

2893
01:35:21,412 --> 01:35:26,195
coming back trying to fit
into contemporary society.

2894
01:35:26,195 --> 01:35:27,463
I liked it.

2895
01:35:27,463 --> 01:35:30,818
"The Second Hundred Years"
was truly vintage Markham.

2896
01:35:30,818 --> 01:35:35,102
And I want everyone out there
right now to write to ABC,

2897
01:35:35,102 --> 01:35:39,526
and say, please, please,
Mr. ABC Executive,

2898
01:35:39,526 --> 01:35:43,120
bring back "The Second Hundred
Years," starring Monte Markham.

2899
01:35:43,120 --> 01:35:46,865
Please do it for the laughter.

2900
01:35:46,865 --> 01:35:49,152
For the love.

2901
01:35:49,152 --> 01:35:50,070
For the boys.

2902
01:35:52,567 --> 01:35:56,032
-Well, Frank that
was very touching.

2903
01:35:56,032 --> 01:35:58,268
I'm sure the "Second
Hundred Year"

2904
01:35:58,268 --> 01:36:00,106
issue was on everyone's mind.

2905
01:36:03,561 --> 01:36:06,307
Shall I crush your skull,
or do you want to do it?

2906
01:36:06,307 --> 01:36:07,655
-Oh, I think you ought to.

2907
01:36:07,655 --> 01:36:08,274
-Very well.

2908
01:36:12,368 --> 01:36:17,970
[CLANG] Well, in the immortal
words of Monte Markham,

2909
01:36:17,970 --> 01:36:20,277
"Here come de judge."

2910
01:36:20,277 --> 01:36:23,692
FRANK: Uh, that was Pigmeat
Markham. [CLANG] Ahh!

